## Why This Is Asked
Designing a modular, policy-driven prompt assembly system tests understanding of multi-tenant safety, latency budgets, and deterministic prompt composition under constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Fragment catalogs with metadata: policy, latency, locale, tone
- Tenant-aware scoring and ordering under latency limits
- Guardrails and watermark injection when needed
- Audit logging with fragment IDs and decision trace

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype outline (not full implementation)
const fragments = [
  {id:'intent', text:'Explain concisely', latency:10},
  {id:'tone', text:'in a formal style', latency:5},
  {id:'locale', text:'English', latency:2}
];
function assemble(tenantPolicy, budget){
  // simplistic greedy selection
  let chosen = [];
  let used= new Set();
  for(const f of fragments){
    if(!used.has(f.id) && budget- f.latency >=0 && tenantPolicy.includes(f.id)){
      chosen.push(f); used.add(f.id);
      budget -= f.latency;
    }
    if(chosen.length>=3) break;
  }
  return chosen.map(x=>x.text).join(' ');
}
```

